> ## Scene 4 – The Sounds of Battle (Lead‑in to Rescue)
> 
> **Purpose:** Tighten tension and let them choose how to approach the rescue.
> 
> **Setup:**
> - The trail bends around a rocky outcrop / dense thicket.
> - They hear clearly now:
>   - A woman’s voice: “You will not burn this grove!”
>   - Human voices: “Bind the witch!” / “For the One Above!”
>   - Crackle of a **fire** that sounds wrong in a living forest.
> 
> **Checks / options:**
> 
> 1. **Approach decision**  
>    Ask directly:
>    - “Do you rush forward?”
>    - “Do you try to sneak closer?”
>    - “Do you hang back and scout?”
> 
> 2. **Stealth / Recon (if they sneak or scout)**
>    - Group Stealth check DC 12 (or individual if only one scouts).
>    - On success: they get to choose a good approach position and maybe act in a surprise round.
>    - On failure: they are noticed as they approach; no surprise, enemies ready weapons.
> 
> 3. **Perception / Insight (quick read of the situation)**
>    - Perception DC 11–12:
>      - Count approximate combatants.
>      - Notice that **the elf druid is outnumbered**.
>    - Insight DC 11–13:
>      - The humans are in “grab and drag away” mode, not “kill on sight.”
>      - The elf is fighting to **protect the grove** and its standing stones, not just herself.
> 
> **Transition:**
> - After this quick mini‑choice and checks, move into the full reveal:
> 
> > “You push aside the last branches and see a small clearing, a half‑circle of ancient stones, and a lone wood‑elf druid, staff in hand, holding her ground as a group of armored humans tries to surround her…”
> 
> → **Scene 5: The Burning Grove – Combat & Rescue**
